WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:28.910
<v Speaker 1>Araudubillahi mina shaytana rajim bismillahir rahmanur raheem Allahumma salli Allah sayyidina Muhammadin wala Ali sayyidina Muhammad kama sallaita wa salamta wa barata wa rahim ta wa tarahamta ala Ibrahim wa ila al Ibrahim fil alamina inna kahamiru majid assalam alaikum wa rahmatullahi wa barakatu So now that we've completed Suratul Fateha I wanted to do a session on this idea of istiqama

2
00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:58.910
<v Speaker 1>which is remaining steadfast on the straight path and this is one of the major challenges of walking the path and it is something that we all probably can relate to we all as Muslims we want to do the right thing we want the straight path but we're not always able to for various different reasons and then we end up getting frustrated with ourselves

3
00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.470
<v Speaker 1>and sometimes with religion itself.

4
00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:08.830
<v Speaker 1>Now this is something that I have personally struggled with extensively in my life.

5
00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:17.950
<v Speaker 1>But then Alhamdulillah I came across some material and some perspectives that has helped me a lot.

6
00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:25.870
<v Speaker 1>And so I thought I would like to share that with you hoping and praying that it will do the same for you.

7
00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:55.230
<v Speaker 1>In Surah Al-Fateha we request Allah subhanahu wa ta'ala for guidance we say guide us towards the straight path now this verse is the essence of the Surah the initial verses they are basically building up to this one right giving us an understanding of who Allah is Alhamdulillah Rabbil Alameena Rahman Rahimaliki Aumideen

8
00:01:56.510 --> 00:02:05.150
<v Speaker 1>but this is it is called the prayer of all prayers.

9
00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:06.750
<v Speaker 1>Guide us, guidance.

10
00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:13.870
<v Speaker 1>Guide us through instructions, role models and then through your divine companionship.

11
00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:19.350
<v Speaker 1>Now the question is what exactly is Sirat al-Mustaqeen?

12
00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:24.030
<v Speaker 1>Allah actually defines it for us in Suratul Yaseen.

13
00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:45.710
<v Speaker 1>where he says and no doubt about it you should worship me for that is the straight path so ibada or ubudiya the willing submission is how Allah defines the straight path

14
00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:58.030
<v Speaker 1>The person who willingly submits himself or herself to Allah is the one who has found the straight path, which Alhamdulillah we all have.

15
00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:02.030
<v Speaker 1>But now the big question is how do we walk the path?

16
00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:08.030
<v Speaker 1>Because we never get to our destination by just finding the correct path.

17
00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:11.150
<v Speaker 1>We get to it by actually walking it, right?

18
00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:13.230
<v Speaker 1>So how do we walk this path?

19
00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:16.190
<v Speaker 1>And what is the term?

20
00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:18.190
<v Speaker 1>Is there a term for walking this path?

21
00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:19.950
<v Speaker 1>There is.

22
00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:28.830
<v Speaker 1>And that term is called Taqwa, which is a strong mindfulness for the presence of Allah in our lives.

23
00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:40.510
<v Speaker 1>Taqwa, commonly translated as piety, is essential for Abu Diyya, for attaining the ranks of servitude.

24
00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:53.710
<v Speaker 1>And that is exactly what we asked for when we said, Iya ka na'budu wa iya ka nasta'in It is only you we worship Again, worship being the straight path.

25
00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:57.870
<v Speaker 1>And it is only you we seek help from.

26
00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:02.350
<v Speaker 1>Ista'ana, right?

27
00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:04.430
<v Speaker 1>What is this method of Ista'ana?

28
00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:06.110
<v Speaker 1>Seeking Allah's help?

29
00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:08.350
<v Speaker 1>Allah explains that in Surah Al-Baqarah.

30
00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:12.030
<v Speaker 1>He says, Wa asta'inu bis sabri wa salat

31
00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:40.910
<v Speaker 1>and seek my help through patience and prayer and the same word is used astainu so we have now just solved this equation right we all desire Sirat al-Mustaqim the straight path Mustaqim as I explained earlier a path that has istaqama built into it right a path of spiritual elevation

32
00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:42.670
<v Speaker 1>How is that achieved?

33
00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:46.470
<v Speaker 1>Through Ubudiyya, willing servitude to the will of Allah.

34
00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:48.910
<v Speaker 1>How do we walk this path?

35
00:04:49.630 --> 00:05:00.270
<v Speaker 1>By developing piety, by developing Taqwa, an acute awareness of who Allah is, by establishing a connection with Him.

36
00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:03.070
<v Speaker 1>And how do we connect with Him?

37
00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:07.550
<v Speaker 1>Bisabri wassalat, through patience and through prayer.

38
00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:10.830
<v Speaker 1>Now we all know about the daily prayers.

39
00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:14.110
<v Speaker 1>But what does Sabr mean here in this context?

40
00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:20.110
<v Speaker 1>It means to be steadfast and consistent with our prayers.

41
00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:34.350
<v Speaker 1>And then the ayat continues to say, And no doubt about it, for sure, this is truly an extremely difficult task for most people.

42
00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:36.270
<v Speaker 1>Illa, except,

43
00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:45.230
<v Speaker 1>those who possess the knowledge and who possess the knowledge and the awareness of the majesty of their master.

44
00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:48.750
<v Speaker 1>People who have Taqwa.

45
00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:57.950
<v Speaker 1>So it all ultimately boils down to consistency and being steadfast, Istaqamah.

46
00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:00.390
<v Speaker 1>Now what is Istaqamah?

47
00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:04.830
<v Speaker 1>It comes from the same root word ama

48
00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:09.950
<v Speaker 1>That was used for Mustaqim, Saratul Mustaqim, the same root word, qama.

49
00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:14.510
<v Speaker 1>So istaqim means to stay firm on something.

50
00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:17.470
<v Speaker 1>And how do we stay firm?

51
00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:25.990
<v Speaker 1>Allah answers that in the Quran when He says, .

52
00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:33.950
<v Speaker 1>He says, establish the prayer, be firm, stay firm on your prayer, stay firm on your salat.

53
00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:44.190
<v Speaker 1>So Istiqamah is to establish something firmly or to be solid in something, to be firm in it.

54
00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:45.870
<v Speaker 1>Right?

55
00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:49.230
<v Speaker 1>So resilience is one of the words that is used.

56
00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:52.750
<v Speaker 1>Grit, some people use the word grit.

57
00:06:54.430 --> 00:07:02.110
<v Speaker 1>But Istiqamah is essentially the ability to stand firm and be consistent in our Iman, in our worship and our belief.

58
00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:11.790
<v Speaker 1>To give you sort of a visual representation from the physical world of the sister ama, think about a solid tree.

59
00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:18.750
<v Speaker 1>A solid tree, it stands firm even when the wind blows, right?

60
00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:22.510
<v Speaker 1>Even if a storm hits it, it stays its ground.

61
00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:29.310
<v Speaker 1>Now, the question we're trying to address is, how can we be like that?

62
00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:42.190
<v Speaker 1>How do we develop the ability to stay strong on this path despite the storms of our situations, of our challenges, of our environments?

63
00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:52.270
<v Speaker 1>So you must have noticed if you read the Quran that in many verses Allah subhanahu wa ta'ala he always mentions two things when he talks about faith.

64
00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:59.630
<v Speaker 1>He always couples Iman with Amal.

65
00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:02.670
<v Speaker 1>He couples faith with action.

66
00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:08.590
<v Speaker 1>So we understand that there are two parts to what we are supposed to do.

67
00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:11.630
<v Speaker 1>There is the inner part and then there's the outer part.

68
00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:13.710
<v Speaker 1>What's the inner part?

69
00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:17.470
<v Speaker 1>It's the belief, the iman, the faith.

70
00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:28.830
<v Speaker 1>Another way we can think about this in terms of ibada is that there is a worship for the heart and then there is a worship for the limbs.

71
00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:32.270
<v Speaker 1>The body, the tongue, the ears, the eyes.

72
00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:33.710
<v Speaker 1>You understand?

73
00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:39.630
<v Speaker 1>There are the internal Ibadat and then there are the external Ibadat.

74
00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:46.190
<v Speaker 1>The internal has to do with our belief system, with our Iman.

75
00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:49.830
<v Speaker 1>How do we think of Allah ?

76
00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:51.150
<v Speaker 1>How do we view Him?

77
00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:57.070
<v Speaker 1>How do we respond internally with the things that He has asked us to do?

78
00:08:57.830 --> 00:08:59.870
<v Speaker 1>and why he has asked us to do those.

79
00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:01.310
<v Speaker 1>That's all internal.

80
00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:05.310
<v Speaker 1>It has to do with our certainty or with our doubt, right?

81
00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:08.670
<v Speaker 1>So certainty is Yaqeen, doubt is Shaq.

82
00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:13.390
<v Speaker 1>So we are either certain about it or we have doubts about it.

83
00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:18.990
<v Speaker 1>But that all has to do with our internal mechanism, our internal worship system.

84
00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:21.390
<v Speaker 1>What are the external Ibadat?

85
00:09:22.390 --> 00:09:26.350
<v Speaker 1>Those are the obvious ones, the five time prayers, reading Quran, you know,

86
00:09:26.830 --> 00:09:32.270
<v Speaker 1>guarding our ears, eyes, all of that, you know, these are all external type of Embadat.

87
00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:40.110
<v Speaker 1>Now, if I asked you, what do you think is essential for Istaqama?

88
00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:43.790
<v Speaker 1>Is it the internal or the external?

89
00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:50.510
<v Speaker 1>What would you say is essential for Istaqama?

90
00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:58.830
<v Speaker 1>Our Prophet sallallahu alaihi wa sallam in a hadith, he stated, Inna fil jasadi mudha.

91
00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:05.470
<v Speaker 1>Isa sulihat salahal jasadu kullu wa iza fasadat fasadal jasadu kullu.

92
00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:11.070
<v Speaker 1>Allah wahi al kalba.

93
00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:16.270
<v Speaker 1>Indeed, within the body, there is a lump of flesh.

94
00:10:17.830 --> 00:10:21.150
<v Speaker 1>Isa saluhat salahal jasadu kullu.

95
00:10:21.230 --> 00:10:22.510
<v Speaker 1>If it is set right,

96
00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:27.070
<v Speaker 1>If it is set straight, then the entire body will be set right.

97
00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:35.150
<v Speaker 1>But if it is corrupted, then the entire body will be corrupted.

98
00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:41.390
<v Speaker 1>Indeed, beware it is the heart.

99
00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:50.430
<v Speaker 1>So what we're learning from this hadith is that the master of the body isn't actually the mind.

100
00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:56.670
<v Speaker 1>Which sounds a bit strange because many people believe that the mind is what is in control.

101
00:10:57.550 --> 00:10:59.310
<v Speaker 1>Well, news flash.

102
00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:02.270
<v Speaker 1>It's actually your heart that is in control.

103
00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:04.750
<v Speaker 1>It's your heart that controls your mind.

104
00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:07.310
<v Speaker 1>Now what do I mean by that?

105
00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:14.350
<v Speaker 1>The type of heart that you have will control your thoughts.

106
00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:19.390
<v Speaker 1>It'll control your mind and eventually it'll control your actions.

107
00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:28.910
<v Speaker 1>So whatever your heart truly desires, your mind will think of ways to fulfill those desires.

108
00:11:29.630 --> 00:11:32.030
<v Speaker 1>And then the limbs will actually make it happen.

109
00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:33.390
<v Speaker 1>They will execute it.

110
00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:36.350
<v Speaker 1>You see?

111
00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:38.670
<v Speaker 1>So the heart is the king.

112
00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:44.750
<v Speaker 1>What the king wants needs to happen.

113
00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:47.070
<v Speaker 1>Who helps the king

114
00:11:48.990 --> 00:12:16.430
<v Speaker 1>figure out how to make it happen that's the wazir that's the mind right that's the brain and then there is the army which is the limbs that actually carry out and execute what the wazir comes up with for the king so the mind actually becomes a tool an instrument to execute whatever is in the heart and this is why our Prophet sallallahu alaihi wasallam he said if you take care of this lump of flesh which is the heart

115
00:12:17.630 --> 00:12:22.110
<v Speaker 1>Now everyone be clear that when I say the heart, I'm not talking about the physical heart, right?

116
00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:24.270
<v Speaker 1>I'm talking about the spiritual heart, the cult.

117
00:12:25.870 --> 00:12:38.430
<v Speaker 1>So if this heart is purified, when you take care of that heart, when you feed the heart, when you rectify the heart, then all your actions will automatically become pure.

118
00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:45.790
<v Speaker 1>But when the heart is corrupted, when it is starved, when it's not taken care of,

119
00:12:46.750 --> 00:13:10.270
<v Speaker 1>when it is deprived of its spiritual sustenance then the rest of the actions will just naturally get corrupted and what's really interesting that if you look at like if you see want to see this theory in its practical form let's look at the chronological theme of revelation

120
00:13:10.910 --> 00:13:13.310
<v Speaker 1>Just think about how the Quran was revealed.

121
00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:16.390
<v Speaker 1>What were the first verses to be revealed?

122
00:13:17.710 --> 00:13:23.470
<v Speaker 1>They were not about drinking alcohol and they were not about preventing people from committing fornication.

123
00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:27.710
<v Speaker 1>They were not about the rules of halal and haram.

124
00:13:30.430 --> 00:13:36.470
<v Speaker 1>The first verses to be revealed were all about building taqwa of Allah Subhanahu wa Ta'ala.

125
00:13:37.070 --> 00:13:58.430
<v Speaker 1>they were all about building an attachment with Allah subhanahu wa ta'ala, about understanding the day of judgment, realizing and learning that we will be taken to account for our actions, that we're not just living in this life without a purpose like animals, that there is a purpose to Allah's tawhee, there is a purpose to Allah's oneness.

126
00:13:59.790 --> 00:14:02.270
<v Speaker 1>So the initial verses of Revelation

127
00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:29.870
<v Speaker 1>were about you know the day of judgment they were about retribution they were about loving Allah they were about understanding what Allah has done for us it was about understanding what Allah wants from us about life and death about you know basically who He is and what He wants why because all those initial verses of Revelation they were building a foundation for us

128
00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:34.190
<v Speaker 1>they were setting the foundation of his taqama.

129
00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:49.710
<v Speaker 1>So now if we go back to the example of the salah tree, Allah subhanahu wa ta'ala says in Suratul Ibrahim, when he gives the example of a good word, he says that a good word is like a good tree.

130
00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:59.870
<v Speaker 1>alam tara kaifadaraballahu mathalan kalimatin tayyibatin ka shajaratin tayyibatin asluha thaabit wa fara uha fissamaa

131
00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:27.870
<v Speaker 1>do you not see how Allah sets forth the example of a good word which is like a fruitful tree its roots are firmly fixed into the earth and its branches rise up to the sky so in other words its foundation is solid that's what the word Allah uses and then its branches rise up towards the sky see you can become elevated

132
00:15:28.750 --> 00:15:46.270
<v Speaker 1>Right, you can grow, you can get higher, you can force yourself into a path of spiritual elevation, but if you don't have solid roots, it's not gonna last long, and this is what happens with so many people that.

133
00:15:47.150 --> 00:16:15.870
<v Speaker 1>and this is something that I have struggled with myself so I can actually speak from experience that I can I have forced myself into the prayers fasting and this and that and I do it for a while and I push myself and I push myself and I just grit my teeth and I do it but doesn't last something happens and suddenly everything goes out the window and this was happening for a while until I finally understood these things because what I realized is that the taller a tree gets the deeper its roots have to go

134
00:16:18.670 --> 00:16:27.550
<v Speaker 1>So if you study the way Allah has designed a tree, you'll see that the taller it is, the more deep and wide its roots are spread within the earth.

135
00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:34.030
<v Speaker 1>In fact, even mountains we know have foundations, and the taller it is, the more it is grounded within the earth.

136
00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:44.390
<v Speaker 1>Everything, anything in existence, in order for it to be solid and firm and to have a taqama, it must have a solid foundation.

137
00:16:46.590 --> 00:16:57.070
<v Speaker 1>And so Allah is telling us, and even in the training that He gives us, He did not start out with the rules, He started out with building our foundation.

138
00:16:58.750 --> 00:17:05.190
<v Speaker 1>And this is why Suratul Fateha, which is essentially the summary of the entire Quran, right?

139
00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:10.190
<v Speaker 1>It's a description of what you are about to find in the remaining 113 surahs.

140
00:17:11.390 --> 00:17:17.230
<v Speaker 1>It begins, look how Surah Al-Fateha begins, by creating an understanding of who Allah is.

141
00:17:17.630 --> 00:17:21.710
<v Speaker 1>Alhamdulillahi Rabbil Alameen, Al-Rahmani Raheem, Maliki Aumideen.

142
00:17:21.950 --> 00:17:26.750
<v Speaker 1>These are all verses about knowing Allah, about setting this foundation.

143
00:17:28.750 --> 00:17:31.150
<v Speaker 1>And once this foundation is built,

144
00:17:31.990 --> 00:18:00.190
<v Speaker 1>which will naturally engender taqwa and tawakkul which is you know our reliance and trust in Allah only then we are taught to ask for the rules and guidance but look at the order of these verses right the beginning verses are designed to build a solid foundation

145
00:18:00.910 --> 00:18:05.070
<v Speaker 1>so that when the rules are revealed, we can just have a stakama.

146
00:18:06.190 --> 00:18:06.870
<v Speaker 1>You understand?

147
00:18:10.190 --> 00:18:21.070
<v Speaker 1>If we look at the example of the tree again, when a wind blows, if the roots of the tree are deep and they are firm within the ground, what happens to the tree?

148
00:18:22.670 --> 00:18:29.630
<v Speaker 1>Well, its leaves might move, the branches might shake and break, but the tree stays standing.

149
00:18:30.110 --> 00:18:30.430
<v Speaker 1>Right?

150
00:18:30.670 --> 00:18:31.550
<v Speaker 1>It's solid.

151
00:18:32.750 --> 00:18:35.710
<v Speaker 1>But what happens to a tree that does not have deep roots?

152
00:18:36.830 --> 00:18:38.350
<v Speaker 1>It easily gets knocked over.

153
00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:49.390
<v Speaker 1>And I believe that the problem with Islamic training these days is that we are raised in such a way that we just don't have solid roots.

154
00:18:51.550 --> 00:18:55.310
<v Speaker 1>Our training method is the rule shaming method.

155
00:18:55.790 --> 00:18:59.870
<v Speaker 1>It's this rule oriented or rule shaming method.

156
00:19:00.670 --> 00:19:08.110
<v Speaker 1>This is the method where you grow up as a kid and the only thing you are really taught about is haram and hellfire.

157
00:19:09.950 --> 00:19:14.110
<v Speaker 1>And the emphasis is on if you do haram, you will go to hellfire.

158
00:19:15.070 --> 00:19:19.870
<v Speaker 1>And that's how many of us were raised and that's how many of us are raising our children.

159
00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:23.790
<v Speaker 1>Well, it is true that there are things that are haram.

160
00:19:24.350 --> 00:19:26.270
<v Speaker 1>It is true that there is hellfire.

161
00:19:26.750 --> 00:19:30.830
<v Speaker 1>It is true that Allah has anger and wrath.

162
00:19:31.310 --> 00:19:32.030
<v Speaker 1>This is all true.

163
00:19:33.310 --> 00:19:37.070
<v Speaker 1>But why are children being taught that above His mercy?

164
00:19:40.030 --> 00:19:43.950
<v Speaker 1>Why are we not teaching our children that Allah is the most merciful?

165
00:19:45.470 --> 00:19:52.670
<v Speaker 1>And that even if you make a mistake, if you make a mistake and you repent, you can always try again.

166
00:19:54.510 --> 00:19:57.150
<v Speaker 1>Why is this not the message that is being taught?

167
00:19:58.910 --> 00:20:04.910
<v Speaker 1>And the answer, unfortunately, is because this is to control the behavior of the child.

168
00:20:05.870 --> 00:20:09.230
<v Speaker 1>So many of us use fear as this control mechanism.

169
00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:13.710
<v Speaker 1>The only problem is that this might have worked

170
00:20:14.350 --> 00:20:23.310
<v Speaker 1>maybe somewhat in my generation, more so in the previous generation, but in the new generation, it just backfires.

171
00:20:25.110 --> 00:20:35.390
<v Speaker 1>When we're using false fear or false shame, unhealthy shame to control someone's behavior, we're not building solid roots.

172
00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:37.870
<v Speaker 1>What we're building is avoidance.

173
00:20:40.430 --> 00:20:48.070
<v Speaker 1>Imagine that you grew up in a household where you're always being told, let's take police as an example, right?

174
00:20:48.070 --> 00:20:50.830
<v Speaker 1>You're always being told that you better watch out for the police.

175
00:20:51.630 --> 00:20:53.070
<v Speaker 1>They're going to throw you in jail.

176
00:20:53.790 --> 00:20:54.910
<v Speaker 1>Don't make a mistake.

